claim:
needs:
title: Merge-safe numbering across branches
desc:27
Sequential numbers collide when two branches allocate at once. Verified: a
branch creating .pidly/5 and another creating .pidly/7/5 merge cleanly and
leave two tickets numbered 5, so find -name 5 has two answers and every
needs: 5 is ambiguous. The lock cannot help — branches do not see each other
until merge.

The real choice is prevention versus detection. Only non-coordinating ids
(hash, timestamp, per-actor prefix) prevent it, and all of them give up the
readable number this project is built around. Everything that keeps "7"
detects instead.

Recommended: a number registry. Allocation writes .pidly/.n/<number>
containing a nonce and the title, never modified afterwards. Two branches
taking the same number produce an add/add conflict on that path, so git
blocks the merge; different numbers merge cleanly. Both verified.

It also makes allocation cheaper — a flat readdir instead of a tree walk —
and guarantees numbers are never reissued even if someone hard-deletes a
ticket directory, which today would recycle the number.

Cost: one small file per ticket in a hidden directory, a second source of
truth for check to verify, and resolution still means renumbering one side.
Detection at merge time is the win, not automatic repair.
